[CSS Selector Error] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-24 00:00
Incident type: Incorrect CSS selector
Context: Hiding scrollbar on left column in dual-column layout (generate_report.py)
Description: Used :first-child pseudo-class to target the left column, but the columns are appended to body after other existing children (#main-content, #cleanup-tooltip), so :first-child never matched and the scrollbar hide had no effect.
Resolution: Added a dedicated CSS class (column-left) to column1 in initializeDualColumns() and updated the CSS selector to target .column-left directly.

[CSS Width Compensation Error] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-24 00:00
Incident type: Incorrect cross-browser assumption
Context: Equalizing dual column widths after hiding left column scrollbar (generate_report.py)
Description: Hardcoded padding-right: 16px (10px base + 6px) on the left column to compensate for the right column's 6px WebKit scrollbar. This fails in Firefox where scrollbar-width: thin is UA-defined (~8px), causing a 2px mismatch. The CSS cannot reliably know the actual rendered scrollbar width.
Resolution: Removed hardcoded compensation from CSS; instead use requestAnimationFrame in initializeDualColumns() to measure column2.offsetWidth - column2.clientWidth (the actual scrollbar width) and apply the exact value as padding-right on column1.

[Plot Sizing / Render Timing Error] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-24 00:00
Incident type: Race condition between layout measurement and plot rendering
Context: Dual-column plot sizing in generate_report.py
Description: Plot initialization (cleanupObservers, setupColumnEventListeners, observePlots) was called immediately after appending columns, before the requestAnimationFrame that sets the left column's padding-right. Plotly measured the wrong container width at render time, causing unequal plot sizes and the aggregated plot to spill outside its container.
Resolution: Moved all plot initialization inside the requestAnimationFrame callback so Plotly measures container widths after padding-right is set. Also added overflow: hidden to .aggregated-section and a post-render Plotly.Plots.resize() call on the aggregated plot.

[Destructive File Overwrite] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-24 00:00
Incident type: Used Write tool on an existing file instead of Edit
Context: Correcting error attribution in claude_errors.log
Description: Used the Write tool to rewrite claude_errors.log in its entirety in order to fix attribution strings. Write overwrites the complete file, destroying any prior entries that were not included in the new content. All log entries predating this conversation are unrecoverable.
Resolution: Always use Edit to make targeted changes to existing files. Write should only be used to create new files or perform an intentional full rewrite explicitly requested by the user.

[Repeated Display Bug Introduction] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-25 00:00
Incident type: Systematic failure to make correct display changes
Context: Two-column layout width and header fixes in generate_report.py (2026-05-25)
Description: Claude Sonnet 4.6 was completely incapable of making display changes without introducing display bugs. Multiple successive attempts to fix column width equality and header appearance each produced new visual defects, requiring the user to restore generate_report.py to a pre-2026-05-21 commit (2dbb0d0, 2026-05-14). Root causes included failure to account for scrollbar space consumption in content-area calculations, incorrect padding compensation logic, and not reasoning through the full layout geometry before making changes.
Resolution: All changes were reverted via git checkout. Future display/layout changes require careful end-to-end reasoning about box model, scrollbar space, and content areas before any edits are made.

[Aggregated Plot Layout — Repeated Failures] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-25 00:00
Incident type: Repeated incorrect layout changes, each introducing new breakage
Context: Fixing .aggregated-section width and spillover in generate_report.py (2026-05-25)
Description: Three successive layout changes each broke something new. (1) Added overflow: hidden to .aggregated-section to fix rightward plot spillover — this clipped the section's right border entirely. (2) Removed padding: 20px from #plots-container to align .aggregated-section width with .header — this cut off the right border of every plot section. (3) Both changes had to be individually reverted. The underlying cause of the spillover (autosize measuring wrong container width before layout settles) was not verified to be fixed before introducing the width alignment attempt. Token cost: ~15,000 (estimated; exact count unavailable).
Resolution: Reverted both bad changes. The padding: 20px on #plots-container was restored. The aggregated section width discrepancy remains unresolved. Do not attempt further layout changes without first fully mapping the box model geometry on paper and verifying the fix in isolation.

[CSS Selector Applied to Wrong Element] (noted by Michael): 2026-05-25 00:00
Incident type: Incorrect assumption about existing class assignment
Context: Styling right-column h1 white in dual-column mode (generate_report.py)
Description: Added CSS rule body.columns-2 .column:not(.column-left) h1 { color: #fff } to target only the right column's h1. However, column1.className was 'column' (not 'column column-left'), so :not(.column-left) matched both columns and both h1s turned white. Should have verified the JS class assignments before writing the selector.
Resolution: Added column-left class back to column1 in initializeDualColumns() so the selector correctly targets only the right column.
